WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.480
From Neo to John Wick viewers just can't
stop hitting pause

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.800
in analyzing frame after frame of Keanu
Reeves movies.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.480
There are too many unforgettable moments
to count throughout his career

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.320
but these are the ones that truly stand
out above the rest.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.880
While the famous proverb states "hell
hath no fury like a woman's scorn,"

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.119
it may have to be updated to say, "hell
hath no fury like John Wick after you

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.160
kill his puppy."
When we meet this former hitman he's

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.440
living a quiet life of retirement while
grieving his late wife.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.560
But after the bloody assault on his new beloved pet
he understandably desires revenge.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.800
So he smashes open his basement floor in order
to access the secret cache of weapons

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.399
he's buried beneath his home
thus irrevocably plunging him back into

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.600
the world of assassins.
This particular scene is frequently

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.920
paused by fans because it makes you
wonder

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.040
what exactly is Wick going to do with
that sledgehammer that he just happened

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.160
to have lying around?
This moment also shows the grief and

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.960
desire for vengeance that consumes him.
Along with the fact that the dog was a

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.840
present from his wife,
whose death left him completely

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.600
shattered, the sledgehammer is the
viewer's cue that this seemingly slow to

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.920
anger guy is about to go ballistic.
The full weight of everything Wick has

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.200
been going through,
loss, sadness, the need for retribution,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.340
makes this clip among the most
pause-worthy of Reeves's entire career.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.680
Who can resist soaking it all in when
someone demonstrates how lethal a pencil can be?

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.159
In John Wick: Chapter 2, the immediate

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.159
aftermath of his takedown of a couple of
assassins in a coffee bar gets us so

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.900
hyped up that we simply must rewind and
repeatedly digest Reeves' awesomeness.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.040
After a multi-million dollar bounty is
taken out on Wick, he's forced to fight

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.479
off a posse of killers who step
up to claim the booty by closing the

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:43.920
open contract on his life.
In this game of assassin, pretty much

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.320
everyone is willing to take a shot for
that much money,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.240
no matter how well respected the target
is. Wick is able to fend off his

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.280
attackers by eliminating them with a
simple pencil,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.580
proving a writing device can indeed be
mightier than a sword.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.080
"He once killed three men in a bar-" "With a pencil. I know. I've heard the story."

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.840
Fans of the Wick franchise might want to take a
break at this moment to drink in the

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.599
full scope of the impressive display of
violence that's just been unleashed.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:12.800
Not only does wick end two men after
he's been wounded

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.280
he adds to his own lore about how he
killed three men in a bar with a pencil.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.200
Witnessing him actually performed this
feat confirms that he's a badass capable

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.720
of turning just about
any seemingly innocuous object into a

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.200
deadly weapon.
The image of him holding the bloody

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.540
pencil after doing the deed
sums up the emotions we experienced while watching it.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.239
Things get even dicier for John Wick in

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:36.879
his third film.
When he finds the elder who's

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.840
essentially the leader of the underworld
he's given a choice

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.200
die immediately or pledge loyalty to the
high table.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.760
He chooses the latter option so he then
removes his finger and gives his wedding

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.000
ring to the elder as a show of fealty.
This is easily one of the coolest scenes

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.760
it demonstrates how Wick's desire for a
peaceful life is often forced to take a

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.240
back seat to the seemingly endless
obligations incurred by his history of

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.580
underworld violence.
So why is this scene worth pausing?

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.160
It's all about the graphic intensity and the
artistic culmination of so many plot elements.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.480
Wick's commitment to honoring the memory

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.159
of his wife, his efforts to get out of
the underworld lifestyle,

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:17.200
and the respect he holds for his allies
at the continental

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.840
all unite at this moment. His severing of
his own finger reminds us that he is a

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.300
man of focus
commitment and sheer will.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.360
Accepting disfigurement
certainly proves just how far he's willing to go.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.159
The scene in the Matrix in which Neo

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:35.680
ducks backwards to avoid gunfire is
definitely iconic.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.040
It's the first image that immediately
springs to mind when you say the phrase

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.120
"dodging bullets". As Neo and trinity
attempt to rescue Morpheus

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.840
they're ambushed by agents on a rooftop.
Agent Jones takes over a nearby body and

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.360
begins the style of weaving and bobbing
around the bullets that has become

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.040
synonymous with the Matrix franchise.
When Jones fires back

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.599
Neo bends over backwards to avoid death
with Reeves delivering arguably the most

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.640
famous
moment of his career. It also represents

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.820
a huge leap forward in cinematic visual
effects.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.680
There are so many reasons to grab the
remote and study this scene

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.080
frame by frame over and over again.
There's the sight of the bullets flying

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.320
across the screen over reeves body,
the way his arms flail in the air as he

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.940
bends backwards,
and the bullet barely grazing his leg.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.040
This moment
also sets us on the path to believe that

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.800
Neo is indeed the chosen one
who will destroy the matrix and free the people once and for all.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.160
In the Force Awakens when Kylo Ren froze

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.479
Poe Dameron's laser with the force
many fans were triumphantly drawn back

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:41.520
into the world of Star Wars.
But that wasn't the first time that a

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.080
main character showcased this ability in
a major sci-fi action flick. As Keanu

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.320
Reeves fans are well aware
Neo had already performed this same feat

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.560
12 years earlier in the Matrix Reloaded.
Before this unforgettable sequence

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.759
happens Neo and company are headed to
the Merovingian

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:01.199
in order to rescue the key maker who can
get them access to the source of the

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:03.840
matrix.
After some hijinks involving the

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.440
Merovingian's wife, Neo manages to rescue
the key maker

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.360
only for the Merovingian to then corner
him. Once some henchman

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.600
opened fire on Neo he calmly puts his
hand up and brings the bullets to a

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.479
standstill.
Thereby providing us with one of the

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:22.160
most unforgettable images in the entire
series of films.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.240
This moment is worth pausing not only
because of Neo's freezing ability

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.400
but also because of how effortlessly he
does it. When the henchmen open fire on

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.479
him he puts one hand out and brings the

00:05:32.479 --> 00:05:36.080
bullets to a standstill
all while maintaining the same calm

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.720
demeanor that he typically wears within
the Matrix.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.880
His laid-back attitude in the face of
death has a huge effect not just on the

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.240
audience
but also the Merovingian upon witnessing

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.080
this display he remarks,
"Okay, you have some skill."

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.060
The image of Neo preparing to face down
Agent Smith one final time in the

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.440
Matrix Revolutions
unquestionably belongs on anybody's

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.240
shortlist of Keanu Reeves most paused
moments.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.880
After finally making it to the Machine
City, Neo encounters the machine's leader

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.440
Deus Ex Machina. He's able to broker a
deal in which he stops Agent Smith from

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.759
taking over the matrix in the physical
world,

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:17.360
while the machine leader spares the free
human city of Zion.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.600
The leader agrees and then Neo is
inserted into the matrix to begin his

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.120
duel with Agent Smith
after smith goes on a monologue about

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.840
how the purpose of life is that it
should end,

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.919
Neo gets up and strikes that famous pose
although this is hardly the first time

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:35.759
that Neo has taken this stance
it rises above all the others thanks to

00:06:35.759 --> 00:06:40.160
the sheer swagger that he displays
despite the long odds that he faces he

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.080
chooses to stand up when Smith
knocks him down and confidently beckons

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.400
at him as if to say
"come at me bro" Neo's willpower

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.360
determination and refusal to stay down
are what make this moment profoundly special.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.280
"Can you feel it Mr. Anderson?"

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:04.040
Keanu Reeves
has starred in his fair share of comedies
but no scene in his career has provided

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.960
more laughs than the one in Always Be
My Maybe.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.420
When he comes waltzing into a high class
restaurant in slow motion while Awolnation's

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.140
"Sail" plays. In this scene Randall Parks Marcus

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.680
and Vivian Banks Jenny have gone
to meet

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.520
Ali Wong's Sasha and her mystery
boyfriend at the restaurant

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.680
after the three gets settled, Sasha's
boyfriend arrives fashionably late

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.240
and it's dramatically revealed that she
is in fact dating Keanu Reeves who is of

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.840
course played by reeves himself.
While reeves screen time in this movie

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.160
amounts to a mere 14 minutes
he makes the most of it he takes all of

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.840
the hyperbole that's been said about him
over the years and pokes as much

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.479
fun at the audience as he does it
himself. As he arrives in slow motion

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:49.680
both Marcus and Jenny's mouths fall wide
open

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.120
which works as a parody of people who
put Reeves on a pedestal.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.160
By having him play into the assumptions
that people have about him

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.140
he lures the audience into laughing at
themselves.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.120
1989's Bill and Ted's Excellent
Adventure

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.520
was Reeves first real blockbuster and
the scene in which the two title

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.199
metal heads discover the existence of
time travel remains one of the most

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:16.080
memorable moments of his entire career.
Reeves Ted and Alex Winter's Bill are

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.520
working on a high school history project
that will determine whether or not they

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.560
pass their class,
and although they don't know it yet it

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.400
will also decide whether their band will
become the basis for a future utopian

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.039
civilization.
While they sit outside a Circle K

00:08:29.039 --> 00:08:31.360
convenience store working on their
assignment

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.479
a strange man named rufus arrives from
the future

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:38.399
to ensure that Bill and Ted succeed in
their mission. His appearance is soon

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:43.839
followed by Bill and Ted from the future
and then hilarity ensues. The look on

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:47.680
Ted's face as he attempts to comprehend
the strange things unfurling in front of

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.839
him
is why this sequence makes it onto our

00:08:49.839 --> 00:08:54.000
list, his exaggerated ignorance adds to
the farcical nature of this

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.760
scene by playing into many of the
stereotypes about dumb high schoolers.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:00.800
Including the number 69 always being on
their minds

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.000
even though this movie was released more
than 30 years ago

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.839
this scene has managed to retain its
most excellent entertainment value by

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.040
appealing to teenagers of subsequent
generations who are still alive and kicking.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.600
"Oh and Ted give my love to the princesses."

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.800
"Who?" "You'll see."

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.519
Few Keanu Reeves scenes are lingered upon
more than the one in

00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:26.959
Speed, in which he hurls himself from a
car onto a bus,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.660
while both vehicles are barreling along
at over 50 miles per hour.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.959
Reeves plays LAPD bomb defusal
specialist Jack Travern,

00:09:34.959 --> 00:09:38.000
who must deal with a man who's
threatening to blow up a bus full of

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.040
people unless he's paid 3.7 million
dollars.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.800
Jack and his partner Harry race to catch
up to the bus and stop the extortionist

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.160
before it's too late.
Once they catch this speeding vehicle

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.800
Jack makes the decision to join the bus
riders

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.160
thus providing us with an all-time
classic action sequence.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:58.240
Jack jumping into a moving bus ranks
among Reeves most paused clips

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.000
because of the shock factor as well as
the bravado that he displays

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.399
the "wow" element in Jack's fearlessness
in a moment of crisis

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.240
gets our hearts racing and set the tone
for what remains to come

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.780
in one of the best action movies of the
90s.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.720
Keanu Reeves taking a stroll through hell in 2005's Constantine, like it's no big deal,

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:22.820
makes for a surreal and totally pause-worthy moment.
When a woman named

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:27.120
Isabel Dotson utters Constantine's name before jumping off a tall building

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.640
her twin sister Angela goes to
Constantine to figure out why Isabelle

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.920
killed herself.
Constantine then decides that the best

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.959
course of action
is for him to transport himself to hell

00:10:36.959 --> 00:10:41.200
to find the answers that Angela seeks.
The image of Constantine walking among

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.800
demons in hell makes for one of the
coolest Keanu Reeves moments

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.959
because the cinematography is simply
amazing. From the howling winds blowing

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:51.839
fire to the general post-apocalyptic
atmosphere,

00:10:51.839 --> 00:10:55.839
the setting alone makes for an
incredible moment.Then the demon zombies

00:10:55.839 --> 00:10:59.440
giving chase to Constantine as he tries
to recover a medical tag

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.440
really hammer in the gravity of the
danger that he's in it all combines for

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.860
a moment that you'll want to slow down
just to soak everything in.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:08.300
"Jesus."

